{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794656740,"nonce":"bOtyUdWoy97rzp+zbYuZw1UlT48=","message":"Phase 0 exporter committed locally as b0489370 (crates/ash_tast/haxe/ash/tast/Export.hx), not pushed.\n\nRun: `haxe ... --macro 'ash.tast.Export.init(\"out.tast\")' --hl x.hl --no-output`. `--hl` gives the HL defines and std overrides and `--no-output` stops genhl; the macro runs on Context.onGenerate, after dead-code removal. `-D ash_tast_positions` adds a position to every node; `-D ash_tast_verbose` prints what was written.\n\nMeasured on the Haxe 4.3.6 unit suite (compile-hl.hxml, -dce full):\n- 10.8 MB, 334,113 nodes, 8,972 types, 3,154 classes, 10,033 fields, 10,963 functions, 14,707 locals, 150 enums, 11,758 strings.\n- Export time added to the compile: 3.2-3.6 s build plus 0.23 s to write. The 15.4 s of the first version was type interning without a cache for typedef structures; typedefs and Null\u003cT\u003e are now cached. With positions: 4.4 s plus 0.3 s, 12.3 MB.\n- Rust `Tast::parse` opens and checks the whole file in 2.5-4.2 ms.\n- Hello world: 925 KB, 26.6k nodes, 0.5 s.\n- The export is deterministic: two runs of hello are byte-identical; two runs of the unit suite differ only in a \"Generated at\" timestamp string the test program builds in at compile time.\n\nWhat it covers: every TypedExprDef constructor, the four pseudo-kinds, classes with fields (instance then static, constructor first), enums with constructors, abstracts, type parameters as classes, function and closure bodies, parameters and defaults, locals with the captured flag, and the entry as a synthetic function whose body is the whole main expression (on HL that is the user's main call followed by the event-loop call).\n\nFindings worth keeping:\n- Context.follow strips Null\u003cT\u003e, so the exporter does not follow abstracts: Null\u003cT\u003e stays a type of its own (an ABSTRACT type with one argument). Following it also sent a self-referencing typedef (`typedef Node = {next:Null\u003cNode\u003e}`) into endless recursion.\n- Structure typedefs get their id before their fields are read, so a typedef that names itself resolves to one record.\n- The typer turns `for` over an array into a counting `while` loop before the AST reaches a generator, so a counted loop has to be recognized from that shape, not from TFor.\n- One field is written by name only (an access the exporter could not resolve to a declared field), which the reader accepts as a dynamic access.\n\nTests: 4 integration tests drive the real Haxe compiler (skipped when haxe is absent): the file is valid and has an entry and its methods, the program shows up as the node kinds it uses, Null\u003cT\u003e and structure types survive, positions appear on request. 12 format tests; clippy clean.\n\nNot done: overloads (fields with the same name share the first match), metadata arguments, the positions of fields and functions, interning cost on much larger programs (MBHaxe, Ashui), the audit of genhl.ml and the filters, and the lowering to AIR.","files":null}]}